1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: At a million settings a second, trying them all would take about five million years.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: But nobody broke Enigma by trying every setting. They found shortcuts, and the first came from Poland.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: In nineteen twenty-nine, Poland's Cipher Bureau trained German-speaking mathematics students in Poznan, in secret.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: Poznan had been German before the First World War.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: Rayefski spotted a German habit. Each message began with its own rotor setting, typed twice, from a start shared that day.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERA: So the first and fourth letters hide the same letter. So do the second and fifth, and the third and sixth.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: From a day's messages, those links join up into loops, which come in pairs of the same length.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: So the lengths of the loops point to the rotor settings, whatever the plugboard does.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: Rayefski still needed the rotors' wiring. Help came from a spy.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: Hans-Thilo Schmidt, at Germany's Cipher Office, sold secrets to the French, including two months of keys from nineteen thirty-two.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: Another puzzle was the keys' wiring. Rayefski guessed plain order, A to A and B to B, and he was right.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: By the end of nineteen thirty-two, he had all three rotors and the reflector, without ever seeing the machine.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: From January nineteen thirty-three, the Poles read Enigma. As the Germans tightened their rules, they kept finding new ways in.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: Sometimes a repeated letter came out the same twice. Zigalski punched sheets with holes wherever that could happen.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: Here are seven, from a simulated day. The one hole left gives away the ring settings.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERA: Yes, they cut them by hand, with razor blades.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: Rayefski also designed the bomba, which was in effect six Enigmas wired together and driven by a motor.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: Then, in December nineteen thirty-eight, the Germans added two more rotors. Three from five made sixty orders, not six.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: Sixty bombas would have cost fifteen times the Bureau's yearly budget for equipment.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: So in late July nineteen thirty-nine, at a secret site outside Warsaw, the Poles showed French and British codebreakers everything.
